.he "lz4 (1)                      9/28/26                      lz4 (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
lz4
- compress a stream into the LZ4 frame format
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
lz4 [ infile [ outfile ] ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Lz4 reads its input and writes it as a single LZ4 frame, the format the
standard lz4 tool produces.  With no argument, or where a name is -, it
reads the standard input and writes the standard output.
.sp
The input is compressed a block at a time with bounded memory, so it may
be arbitrarily large.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The one narrowing from standard LZ4 is the match window: an offset never
exceeds 16383 bytes, so the decoder's history ring fits a 64K address
space.  A stream written here is read by unlz4, by the standard lz4 tool,
and by mxunlz4 on the host.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
unlz4 (1), tar (1)
